Z||000007||Nashco||12-03-2001||03:11 AM||||Well, I think it's damn cool.  Think of the technology bundled inside of that little thing!  To some this may not seem like much of a product, but nobody can deny that technology is improving at a fantastic rate.<P>The short article has already covered most of the major "what if" points, explaining that the Segway has been designed to deal with it.<P>You can see that this is no chump-change operation, and if you can think it up, they already did and worked out any associated bugs.  Also, this is the first model...just like the first model wheel, buggy, car, etc. it will get better.<P>Just imagine where things can go from here!  This AMAZING technology (the balancing technology in particular) can spurn a whole new drive for all products.  What could you do with a motorcycle employing the same technology...could you lean it to its maximum physical limits and keep it there while riding...it would automatically adjust according to speed and weather conditions..??  What about a pogo stick that won't tip over, how cool would that be?  A ladder that won't tip over?  Stemming from the wheelchair idea...a vehicle that parapalegics could use to play basketball "just like the other people"??  What about installing similar technology in the god-awful handling SUVs, so if they reach certain pre-set conditions, they will use similar motors and controllers to keep the car from tipping and adjust the  vehicle accordingly.  How could this be applied to airplanes to prevent or get them out of uncontrolled spins?<P>Things like that are what this product could be based on, if not it's current use.  I think as is, it DOES have a large demand with time.  The costs are high as it is now, but this will change with time, just like almost everything.<P>I think it's awesome...what do you guys think?<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<BR>*edited typo*<p>[This message has been edited by Nashco (edited 12-03-2001).]||||reg||1
