Z||000019||Tigger||12-03-2001||10:20 AM||||I saw "it" this morning as well.  With an estimated price tag of $3000 I don't see it "rolling off."  I think $1000 would be more reasonable.  The inventor is trying his best to make it seem acceptable for use on the sidewalk.  In some cities you can't get about on anything with wheels on the sidewalk so he's got a big challenge there.  I'd hate to see a collision or watch it mow someone over.  I think it's use in warehouses and the mail service seems more appropriate, and it looks like the inventor is targeting on those markets.<P>I think it's neat, I'd rather have a fold-up electric/pedal powered bicycle.||||reg||1
Z||000020||Cheever3000||12-03-2001||11:24 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Developed at a cost of more than $100 million,<HR></BLOCKQUOTE> He's going to lose a bundle!  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>And it can carry the average rider for a full day, nonstop, on only five cents' worth of electricity.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE>What about me & stimpy & all the people who weigh twice that much?  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Cars are great for going long distances," Kamen says, "but it makes no sense at all for people in cities to use a 4,000-lb. piece of metal to haul their 150-lb. asses around town." In the future he envisions, cars will be banished from urban centers to make room for millions of "empowered pedestrians"--empowered, naturally, by Kamen's brainchild.<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE>There already <B>is</B> an invention "to haul their 150-lb. asses around town" - it's called a <B>bus</B>, hop on it!<P>Good grief!<P><p>[This message has been edited by Cheever3000 (edited 12-03-2001).]||||reg||1
