Z||000035||Standard||12-03-2001||10:43 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by WampusCat:<BR><B>They said that hopefully in a year or so, the cost would be down in the "three thousands."  Why not just save $2500 and get one of these: <A HREF="http://www.zapworld.com/zappy.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.zapworld.com/zappy.htm</A> </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Those zap scooters rule!  The bike shop I work at carries 'em.  Very good way to put some excitment in a boring winter day.  We usually set up a race course around the store and have at it.  Pretty fast little buggers, too.  12-15mph.  Although I've heard there's some people out in Cali modding 'em up to 60mph.  <BR><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic_full.jpg">          <BR><FONT COLOR="red">1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed</FONT><BR><FONT SIZE="1"><FONT COLOR="white">1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="blue">AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus</FONT></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000036||deceler8||12-04-2001||09:24 AM||||The scooter is nothing more than a test bed for a lot of technologies that will find their way into products we will all eventually use.<P>The logical next step is a car-type product that uses these technologies. Imagine how much cheaper, smaller, and yet roomier a car could be if it only required two wheels to operate. Imagine how much smoother takeoffs and landings would be in a plane if it employed these technologies to control balance or stability. <P>IT won't revolutionize the world overnight, but over time it will. If these things do crack the $1000 barrier, I'll be lining up to get one.||||reg||1
