Z||000041||NOCONES||12-05-2001||02:09 AM||||The technology it uses isn't really new and revolutionairy.. it can't keep you or it' self from falling.. it can try, but like any self balancing object it has limits.  At Engineering open house we saw a demo of something very similar to this about 5 years ago.  it was a 1ft tall twowheeled contraption that would sit ther and move the bottom back and forth to keep the top in the air.. the only problem was if you pushed it to far the thing would fall over.  All this thing does is use gyros to control wheels to keep it vertical.. ther isn't any new revolutionary technology there.  The whole thing works just like when you try to balance a baseball bat on your hand.. you let the top lean forward a little, and run to catch op trying to keep the top in the air.. but if you let it go to far, or slow down too quickly.. the whole thing comes down.  I really don't see how this thing cost 100 million to develop especialy considering it was an offshoot of his two wheeled wheel chair.. i ask again what did he do with the other 99million dollars.???<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000042||Oreif||12-05-2001||08:54 AM||||Gyro's were used in WWII tanks to keep the barrel steady while the tank is moving. So he made them smaller and put them on a 2-wheeled cart. Maybe I should write him. It seems it would be better if it had a clear/tinted cover over it for rainy days. I'll tell him I need $50 million up front to design it. I can "design" some "space-age" tinted acrylic that will look like glass but be plastic! Been working on it for years. Think he'll go for it?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <P>------------------<BR>             <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">           <BR><FONT SIZE="1"><B><I>Happiness isn't around the corner...<BR>Happiness IS the corner.</I></B></FONT>||||reg||1
